From df4d92aff4b2bbbc01667f67638b90c64ecbf337 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 23:28:38 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: batch Linux QUIC UDP traffic with optional segmentation
 offload

Use bounded recvmmsg/sendmmsg batches with one reused per-thread receive
buffer set, preserving UDP and destination-address boundaries. Probe
UDP_SEGMENT and split back into original datagrams if offload is unsupported.
Keep GRO disabled and guard kernel-specific APIs/constants. Wire the GSO
option through listener and outbound sockets.

Extract the existing one-packet ngtcp2 scheduler and aggregate at most ten
naturally full packets within its send quantum, without forced padding.
Preserve stream priorities and sent-byte accounting. Honor ngtcp2's generated
path for validation traffic instead of the original remote tuple; mark this
narrow W4 repair for owner-folding in final integration.

Combined native daemon/unit build passes 15 actions. On macOS, 31 selected
cases pass 13366 assertions, including segmentation fallback, one-megabyte
option on/off transfers and deterministic same-IP NAT port rebinding on one
TLS session. Basic 19.38s and limits 3.45s functionals pass first run. Linux
branches, actual GSO execution, Guix and CPU/loss/packetization gates remain
unvalidated. Cross-IP admission/permission transfer remains an integration
gap. No rendezvous, broad sweep, benchmark or default enablement was added.
